<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huelva</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Onoba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huelva is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odiel">Odiel</a> and Tinto rivers meet, and capital of the province of the same name. As ancient Onoba it was a major centre of the Tartessian world, where Phoenician and Greek traders obtained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from the mines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-tinto">Río Tinto</a> area. The province, bordering Portugal, remains a mining and farming region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huelva.jpg" alt="Huelva" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jose A. · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huelva_(Spain)_(41302627111)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Huelva lies in the south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> on the Gulf of Cádiz, facing the Atlantic. It stands on the estuary formed where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odiel">Odiel</a> and Tinto rivers meet, between the two rivers. In ancient times the estuary was rather wide, and it has progressively silted up.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Historians long held that Huelva began as a native Tartessian settlement. Finds of Phoenician material at the Méndez Núñez-Las Monjas site led to the Phoenician chronology being reassessed, and the evidence now favours seeing Huelva as a very early Phoenician colony, founded roughly in the 9th century BCE, though a 10th-century date has also been argued. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> called it ʿunʿu baʿl, '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>'s fort', which Greek texts corrupted to Onoba. The outpost exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, purple dye and salted fish and was a node on routes linking the Atlantic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The Tartessian world entered a crisis in the 6th century BCE, and the town passed slowly to the Turdetani.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> it was Onoba or Onuba and had a mint. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-nusayr">Musa ibn Nusayr</a>'s troops seized it by April 712, and its name became Walba. It was a short-lived independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> from 1012 to 1051, was then annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> in 1091. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a> took it in 1262.</p><p>Huelva was a leading fishing town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> in the 16th century, became a provincial capital in 1833 and a city by royal decree on 17 September 1876. Mining by Rio Tinto, the main company from about 1873, sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and pyrite to its port. On 4 February 1888 soldiers fired on demonstrators against ore-smelting pollution at Rio Tinto, and historians estimate 100 to 200 deaths.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Huelva is the capital of its province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, with a population of 149,410 at the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its port still handles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and pyrite from mines inland. Recreativo de Huelva, founded in 1889 by workers of Rio Tinto, is the oldest football club in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the Fiestas Colombinas honour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>'s departure from the port on 3 August 1492.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huelva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Strabo and Pliny describe Celtici in the south-west, between the Guadiana and the Atlantic and in the Beturia region of Extremadura and <b>Huelva</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/celtici">Celtici</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1012, during the collapse of caliphal power, the Arab family of the Banu Bakr made themselves independent in <b>Huelva</b> and the island town of Saltés at the mouth of the Odiel and Tinto rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-huelva">Taifa of Huelva</a></li><li><blockquote>Tartessos emerged in the 9th century BCE in the region of <b>Huelva</b>, Seville and Cádiz, where a local Late Bronze Age society came into intense contact with Phoenician traders from Gadir.</blockquote><a href="/state/tartessos">Tartessos</a></li><li><blockquote>The small realm covered Niebla, <b>Huelva</b> and the lower Guadiana region, protected by Niebla's massive walls.</blockquote><a href="/state/third-taifa-of-niebla">Third Taifa of Niebla</a></li><li><blockquote>The Turdetani appear after the decline of Tartessos around 500 BCE, occupying the same rich lower Guadalquivir valley in modern Seville, <b>Huelva</b> and Cádiz.</blockquote><a href="/state/turdetani">Turdetani</a></li></ul></section>
